Arguments as supplied: `$ARGUMENTS`

Parsing rule:
- If the argument is `auto` (default when absent or empty), use Form 1.
- Otherwise, the argument is the crew name `NAME` (Form 2).
- OpenCode hands the argument over as one quoted string: strip one pair of surrounding quotes first.

This command resolves staffing only. It has exactly two forms, chosen from
the arguments:

1. The argument is the literal word `auto` (`/crew auto`):

   - From the task the user has already planned in this conversation,
     determine the canonical class role (one of `impl`, `plan`, `review`,
     `judge`, `prose`) and the canonical five-segment class string
     `role/oracle_type/domain_tags/size_band/language` (for example
     `impl/deterministic/none/s/python`). Use only facts already established
     in the plan; never fabricate task facts. If the role or class cannot be
     determined from the plan, ask the user instead of guessing.
   - Run exactly:

     lee-llm-router staff --mode auto --role <role> --class <class>

2. The argument is a crew name (`/crew NAME`):

   - Run exactly:

     lee-llm-router staff --mode crew <name>

Then display the staffing block the command printed to stdout to the user,
verbatim.

This is resolve-only. Do not dispatch anything, do not invoke any provider
binary (claude, codex, agy, opencode, omp, pi, or any other), do not start a
provider run, and do not write, edit, or delete any file.

Finally, offer — do not execute — the dispatch command for the user to run
themselves, filling the placeholders from the staffing block and the packet
file the user wants to send:

lee-llm-router run --role <role> --class <class> --packet <path>

and, when the staffing block names a selected route, offer the explicit
route form instead:

lee-llm-router run --route <route-id> --role <role> --class <class> --packet <path>
